1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:05,640
Okay, so for today's lecture we are going to focus on plant containment.

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:11,600
So under some circumstances you may have to resort to plant containment.

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:21,600
The term plant containment pertains to containing a plant or germ plasm in a facility and preventing

4
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,860
access to that facility from the external environment.

5
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:31,660
So you do not permit the plant to be shifted out from that environment until it is basically

6
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:43,740
resolved to be free of pathogen or free of genetically modified material.

7
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:48,220
So why does this concept of plant containment come into the picture today?

8
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:55,060
This is because certain processes have necessitated the need to contain plants.

9
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:57,940
For example, germ plasm is imported.

10
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:01,540
You import germ plasm from another country.

11
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:05,820
Along with that germ plasm for instance, you bring plant, planting material, it will carry

12
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:08,660
its own repertoire of pathogens.

13
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:15,420
It may contain fungi, it may contain insects, it may contain some element which when you

14
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:20,740
transfer into your environment they will have impact on the crops.

15
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:23,580
And this can lead to economic losses.

16
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:25,340
That is one aspect of containment.

17
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:30,260
The second reason why we need to contain is because of legal requirements such as the Malaysian

18
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:31,260
biosafety laws.

19
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:38,080
For instance, if you genetically modify a plant prior to release you need to obtain permission.

20
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:43,940
So we cannot basically transfer a plant from a laboratory into the field directly.

21
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:50,220
We need to first confine it to contain it within a facility where we carry out the

22
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:52,060
requisite due diligence.

23
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:56,220
We should ensure for instance that the germ plasm from that plant will not be transferred

24
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:59,020
into endemic crops.

25
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:06,980
Sometimes plants may have pollen which will be transferred to land races or the crops

26
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:09,740
which are extent in that particular region.

27
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:15,900
And then you will have a hybrid, there is a likelihood that this hybrid may be genetically

28
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:21,700
modified, which means it may contain pollen from the GM plant as well as the traditional

29
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:22,700
germ plasm.

30
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:28,380
And then you will have problems with regard to the legal aspect of breeding.

31
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:35,620
For instance, local farmers can resort to legal action over your crop because they will

32
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:40,860
say that our crop is organic and yours is genetically modified, ours is traditional

33
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:44,180
and you have interfered with our traditional process.

34
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:47,020
So that is the reason why you need to resort to containment.

35
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:54,380
So in addition to the science behind it, we also have to look at the legal aspect of

36
00:02:54,380 --> 00:03:02,840
compliance with national laws.

37
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:11,980
So the objective of this module is to introduce you to the concept of plant containment, to

38
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:18,380
introduce you to relevant situation where you need plant containment because plant containment

39
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:24,220
is an expensive process, it involves facilities, it involves personnel and it involves training.

40
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:29,220
So in some instances there is no need for containment, for instance it is a native plant and then

41
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:33,060
you culture it, cultivate it and you do not carry out any modification, there is no need

42
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:34,060
for containment.

43
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:39,260
However, when it is a new novel variety or an imported variety you need to resort to

44
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:40,460
containment.

45
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:46,580
So there are procedures associated with containment which we need to adhere to, this may be national

46
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:50,940
or international procedures and we also have design considerations.

47
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:55,820
So at the end of this module I will introduce you to a conceptual transgenic containment

48
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:56,820
facility.

49
00:03:56,820 --> 00:04:02,380
That is the design concept on which our facility is based, we have a transgenic containment

50
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:08,280
facility so it is based on that concept.

51
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:11,560
So these are your learning outcomes.

52
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:15,880
You should be able to describe situations in which you need to apply containment within

53
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:20,880
the context of objective number 2 or learning outcome number 2 which is the relevant national

54
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:27,840
laws which is pertained to living modified organisms and you should be able to understand

55
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:33,180
national policies and guidelines and develop a conceptual design.

56
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:36,040
So these are the key learning outcomes.

57
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:40,480
But one of the outcomes which I like to emphasize is the process of risk assessment.

58
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:46,280
When you refer to learning outcome number 2 you actually have to go through a process

59
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:51,200
of risk assessment, risk mitigation and performance assessment.

60
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:55,160
This process forms the basis of biological risk management.

61
00:04:55,160 --> 00:05:02,280
We will go through that during the course of the lecture.

62
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:05,060
So when do we need to resort to containment?

63
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:11,640
As I explained to you one of the aspects is transgenic plants.

64
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:16,680
That is a definite area in which you need to resort to containment.

65
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:22,060
Sometimes the manufacturers or producers of genetically modified plant may not want their

66
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:28,800
germplasm to escape not because it is a problem to the local varieties but because it was

67
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:29,800
a patent.

68
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:35,040
For instance you have developed a synthetic gene and if that transgene escaped somebody

69
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:40,680
can copy that gene and they can produce a variant of it.

70
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:43,360
So this is why the legal aspect comes into the picture.

71
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:48,840
It is the legal protection of the environment as well as the company which produces that

72
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:54,510
particular germplasm.

73
00:05:54,510 --> 00:05:58,990
So when we refer to containment there are terms associated with containment.

74
00:05:58,990 --> 00:06:01,710
The first term is primary containment.

75
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:11,190
So assuming that barrier, the rectangle is the germplasm which you need to contain.

76
00:06:11,190 --> 00:06:17,550
Primary containment refers to the protection of the personnel.

77
00:06:17,550 --> 00:06:18,550
So that is primary.

78
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:21,350
So you are the personnel associated with the germplasm.

79
00:06:21,350 --> 00:06:27,190
For instance the germplasm may be modified for instance a plant modified to carry transgenic

80
00:06:27,190 --> 00:06:28,190
virus.

81
00:06:28,190 --> 00:06:30,430
So it is a virus.

82
00:06:30,430 --> 00:06:32,710
So you need to protect yourself from that virus.

83
00:06:32,710 --> 00:06:35,230
Maybe the virus is a human pathogen.

84
00:06:35,230 --> 00:06:36,750
So you need to have primary containment.

85
00:06:36,750 --> 00:06:44,110
So anything that which protects you from the what is known as the biological organism

86
00:06:44,110 --> 00:06:46,310
is termed as primary containment.

87
00:06:46,310 --> 00:06:50,590
So primary containment can pertain to the usage of personnel protective equipment.

88
00:06:50,590 --> 00:06:57,230
For instance your respirator, your masks, your gloves all these are primary containment.

89
00:06:57,230 --> 00:07:03,710
In addition to that we have what we term as secondary containment.

90
00:07:03,710 --> 00:07:07,830
Secondary containment pertains to the environment.

91
00:07:07,830 --> 00:07:14,670
So in order to have a watertight containment facility you have to ensure that you have

92
00:07:14,670 --> 00:07:20,230
primary containment which will protect the personnel and secondary containment which protects

93
00:07:20,230 --> 00:07:21,830
the environment.

94
00:07:21,830 --> 00:07:25,150
So secondary containment generally refers to the structure.

95
00:07:25,150 --> 00:07:30,150
For example our transgenic plant containment facility is a facility.

96
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:35,350
So it is a physical structure, it is an engineering control which protects the environment from

97
00:07:35,350 --> 00:07:42,630
any pollen or any pathogens which may be released as a consequence of accidents.

98
00:07:42,630 --> 00:07:50,710
So when you have a breach of this containment, for example there is a barrier, for example

99
00:07:50,710 --> 00:07:55,790
there may be a natural disaster in your glass panel breaks, a glass house.

100
00:07:55,790 --> 00:07:57,550
So you have a breach of containment.

101
00:07:57,550 --> 00:08:03,350
Then you have to resort to certain measures to contain that breach.

102
00:08:03,350 --> 00:08:08,750
So it may involve for example notification informing the authorities that you have a breach.

103
00:08:08,750 --> 00:08:16,910
It may involve collecting all the plants within that radius of their facility and decontaminating.

104
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:20,790
So there are various procedures which need to be followed when you have a breach of

105
00:08:20,790 --> 00:08:21,790
containment.

106
00:08:21,790 --> 00:08:26,270
So you have primary containment, secondary containment and you can have a breach of

107
00:08:26,270 --> 00:08:37,020
containment when any of these barriers have been broken down.

108
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:41,200
These are the key definitions.

109
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:49,000
So it protects laboratory users, primary containment protects laboratory users and secondary containment

110
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,160
protects the environment from the biological agent.

111
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:58,120
In this case the biological agent is the plant which is genetically modified or which has

112
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:05,600
been imported and contains, may contain pathogens.

113
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:08,080
These are the situations which require containment.

114
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:13,280
You have genetically modified plants, imported planting material, plants which have been

115
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:19,800
cultured in vitro in the tissue culture system in which you need to transfer into the field.

116
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:20,800
Challenge test.

117
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:26,120
Challenge test refers to a situation in which you want to test a plant for its ability

118
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:29,240
to resist pathogens from this.

119
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:34,040
In this case you have your plant in a contained facility and you expose it to the fungus,

120
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:38,880
the pure culture of the fungus and you test for the resistance.

121
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:43,560
So in this case you have to be careful with reference to the fungus.

122
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:46,880
You should ensure the fungus is not transferred out of the facility.

123
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:50,960
So in this case you need to resort to containment.

124
00:09:50,960 --> 00:09:54,320
And the fifth one is controlled breeding experiments.

125
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:57,640
Sometimes you want to create a hybrid plant.

126
00:09:57,640 --> 00:10:02,040
So you have crossbred two pure varieties to create a hybrid.

127
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:06,400
However you want to ensure that no external pollen comes into the system.

128
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:09,960
So in this case you have to resort to containment.

129
00:10:09,960 --> 00:10:15,040
So containment does not necessarily pertain to genetically modified plant, it also pertains

130
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:25,080
to controlled breeding experiments as well.

131
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:30,400
Genetically modified plants can have an impact on pollinators.

132
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:34,840
For instance if you have insecticidal proteins in certain plants you have modified them to

133
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:37,800
carry proteins which are insecticidal.

134
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:44,800
If these pollen escapes and contaminates local plants, what are known as local land races,

135
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:50,480
there is a likelihood that those plants may also carry this insecticidal protein genes.

136
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:57,760
And they can disrupt the local ecology which is why we need to have stringent regulations

137
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:06,540
with regard to genetically modified organisms.

138
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:12,060
In some instances you may need to quarantine imported planting material.

139
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:20,540
For instance if you import oil palm seedlings from West Malaysia or from Indonesia they

140
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:24,860
may carry with them pathogens.

141
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:29,180
These pathogens may not manifest themselves at earlier stages, they may manifest themselves

142
00:11:29,180 --> 00:11:30,620
at later stages.

143
00:11:30,620 --> 00:11:37,300
So in order to ensure that these pathogens do not propagate through your ecosystem you

144
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:41,020
need to contain these plants for a limited period of time.

145
00:11:41,020 --> 00:11:44,940
And then you need to analyze, for example you can do molecular tests to determine whether

146
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:46,940
they are carrying pathogens.

147
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:50,340
So if they carry pathogens you basically restrict their import.

148
00:11:50,340 --> 00:12:00,470
In future instances you can screen for those pathogens.

149
00:12:00,470 --> 00:12:05,550
Plants developed in vitro may also be subject to containment.

150
00:12:05,550 --> 00:12:10,590
If they sometimes contain, for instance plants which are developed in vitro are weaker, may

151
00:12:10,590 --> 00:12:12,990
be weaker due to the process of in vitro cultivation.

152
00:12:12,990 --> 00:12:18,390
When you plant them in the field they are likely to be infected with pathogens more

153
00:12:18,390 --> 00:12:22,190
easily as compared to the traditional varieties.

154
00:12:22,190 --> 00:12:26,230
So in this case you may have to resort to containment to protect the plant from

155
00:12:26,230 --> 00:12:27,950
the pathogens.

156
00:12:27,950 --> 00:12:32,550
In some cases for example in the case of banana which has been tissue cultured, a large

157
00:12:32,550 --> 00:12:38,990
number of banana plants may get infected with virus because you are using monoculture.

158
00:12:38,990 --> 00:12:44,150
So if you resort to tissue culture you need to ensure that the plants are resistant before

159
00:12:44,150 --> 00:12:45,990
you transfer them in the field.

160
00:12:45,990 --> 00:12:49,030
Otherwise you will not only create a problem for your crop, you will create a problem

161
00:12:49,030 --> 00:12:54,070
for neighboring crops as well, your neighborhood farming communities.

162
00:12:54,070 --> 00:13:00,280
Then you have control breeding experiment.

163
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:04,880
So sometimes farmers want to develop what are known as determinate hybrids.

164
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:10,040
Determinate hybrid is where you have to pure parental genotypes and you develop a hybrid.

165
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:14,440
So in this case they are very particular with regard to pollen coming in.

166
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:16,880
They do not want other pollen to come in.

167
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:19,560
So in this case we have control breeding experiment.

168
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:24,280
So these experiments are usually done under containment which is why what are known as

169
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:30,240
determinate hybrid crops are very expensive because they require extensive usage of facility

170
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:32,120
for development.

171
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:35,640
You cannot have other pollen coming in.

172
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:40,840
If you have other pollen coming in you will have a problem with the output, the seed.

173
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:46,160
So when you have F1 determinate hybrid variety which is advertised by the producer it has

174
00:13:46,160 --> 00:13:49,560
to contain the germ plasm from the two original parents.

175
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:52,960
You cannot have external pollen coming in.

176
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:57,680
So that's why the farmer pays more for these determinate hybrids where he gets a uniform

177
00:13:57,680 --> 00:14:03,290
crop.

178
00:14:03,290 --> 00:14:05,370
And sometimes we do challenge test.

179
00:14:05,370 --> 00:14:11,130
Challenge test for example if you have a particular variety of fungus, for example rice blast

180
00:14:11,130 --> 00:14:15,770
you may have a fungus which you isolate from the field and then you want to test

181
00:14:15,770 --> 00:14:18,250
these new varieties of rice.

182
00:14:18,250 --> 00:14:22,890
You import different seeds from different countries and you want to test which is the best

183
00:14:22,890 --> 00:14:25,650
stream for growing locally.

184
00:14:25,650 --> 00:14:28,810
But before that you need to subject it to pathogen test.

185
00:14:28,810 --> 00:14:33,970
So you do a challenge test which means you contain the plants in a facility and then

186
00:14:33,970 --> 00:14:36,050
you subject them to the fungus.

187
00:14:36,050 --> 00:14:40,930
You culture pure culture of the fungus and you test which variety is the most suited

188
00:14:40,930 --> 00:14:43,050
for with regard to that fungus.

189
00:14:43,050 --> 00:14:46,090
So the one which resists the fungus you can transfer into your feed.

190
00:14:46,090 --> 00:14:50,830
So this is a challenge test which require containment because if you have for instance

191
00:14:50,910 --> 00:14:54,430
in a containment facility you can control the pathogen.

192
00:14:54,430 --> 00:14:59,630
But suppose you have a breach, the pathogen can spread all over and can then spread to

193
00:14:59,630 --> 00:15:05,490
the local land races and local crops.

194
00:15:05,490 --> 00:15:08,890
So that's the basis for containment.
